# Shell scripts run inside Linux containers (Docker entrypoints) or are
# piped straight into `sh` — a CRLF-mangled shebang breaks both. Force LF
# regardless of core.autocrlf on a Windows checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The text formats the toolchain reads as DATA, not prose. A CRLF checkout
# (core.autocrlf=true, the Windows default) leaves a trailing \r on every line,
# and the parsers that read these files split on "\n" or compare bytes — so the
# \r silently becomes part of a city name, a metro label or a JSON fixture. The
# loaders that already tolerate it say so in their comments ("tolerate a CRLF
# checkout"); these rules stop the problem at the source, so a fresh checkout on
# Windows does not need that tolerance in the first place.
#
# *.go is here for the same reason the AGENTS.md asks for `gofmt -l .` to print
# nothing: gofmt reports every CRLF Go file as unformatted.
#
# `text` normalises to LF in the index and `eol=lf` puts LF in the working tree.
# On Linux and macOS both are already the case, so this changes nothing there.
*.go text eol=lf
*.tsv text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
